    historyContent: {
      origins: "Originating in Austria as an evolution of 2-player Schnapsen (and the earlier 66), Bauernschnapsen adapted the 'short deck' mechanics to a four-player format. It emerged as a distinct social tradition in Austrian rural communities ('Bauern' meaning farmers) where partnership play was preferred for social gatherings.",
      originCountries: [
        'Austria',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "18th Century: Early 'Sixty-Six' games spread across the Habsburg Empire.",
        '19th Century: Schnapsen stabilizes as the primary Austrian variant.',
        'Early 20th Century: Bauernschnapsen (Viererschnapsen) becomes the standardized partnership format in Alpine regions.',
        "Present: One of Austria's most popular card games, with dedicated tournaments and regional clubs.",
      ],
      evolution: 'The game evolved from a simple point-race into a complex bidding game where the value of game points depends on the margin of victory (Schneider/Schwarz).',
      cultural: "Regarded as a test of mental arithmetic and partnership intuition. It is a staple of 'Wirtshaus' (tavern) culture in Austria, Bavaria, and South Tyrol.",
